module gunbc.floor_cost_distribution

import std.types { Bool, Int, String, NonEmptyStr }
import std.measure { Millisecond, millisecond, millisecond_count }

// THE DERIVATIONS BEHIND docs/plans/floor-cost-distribution-near-the-ceiling.md, SO THAT DOCUMENT
// CITES A PRODUCER INSTEAD OF CARRYING COPIED FIGURES.
//
// DESIGN section 6: name the instrument, never transcribe its output -- and if a measurement is
// worth re-deriving it is worth an entry point. The first version of that memo named
// `gunbc.witness_floor_workflow`, which produces the RAW ROWS and performs none of the analysis
// below; the ten-run intersection, the band histogram, the counterfactual replay and the inflation
// percentiles existed only as prose plus numbers. Review 58983 refused it on exactly that ground
// and was right to: a reader could check the arithmetic of nothing.
//
// EVERY FUNCTION HERE IS PURE AND TAKES ITS ROWS AS AN ARGUMENT. Reading the artifacts off disk is
// a separate fact and lives in `tools.floor_cost_distribution_instrument`, which is the §3 split
// between the interface (what the analysis IS) and the realization (where the bytes come from).
// The consequence that matters for evidence: every derivation below is witnessable on a hand-built
// fixture with no run artifacts present, which is what
// `test.claim.floor_cost_distribution_witness` does.
//
// THE INPUT IS THE `required-floor-claim-cost` ARTIFACT that every required-floor run uploads --
// the COMPLETE executed population, not the 25-row `[over-cost]` preview the job log prints.

// `eval_steps` IS THE ONE COLUMN THIS ANALYSIS NEEDS THAT IS NOT A CLOCK. Evaluator steps are
// counted by the interpreter and netted by the same shared-fill rule the CPU figure is, so a row's
// step count is a property of what it evaluated and carries no term for the machine it evaluated
// on. It is what lets `identity_inflation_permille_at_equal_work` below separate "this row got
// more expensive" from "this row got inflated" without choosing a control population at all.
//
// IT IS NOT A WAY AROUND THE CENSORING, and reaching for it as one is the tempting move once the
// clocks below become a coproduct. An interrupted row's step count is QUANTISED BY THE DEADLINE
// POLL -- the poll fires every 1024 steps, so the recorded figure is itself a lower bound rounded
// to a poll boundary, while a completed row's is a true count. So steps are censored on exactly
// the same rows the clocks are, by the same mechanism, and a step-denominated statistic over a
// mixed population has the same defect in a less visible field.

// THE COST IS A COPRODUCT, NOT A NUMBER, AND THIS MODULE IS WHY THE CLASS WAS FILED.
//
// This row used to carry `cpu_ms: Millisecond` for every artifact row. The artifact's population
// includes claims a SAFETY DEADLINE PREEMPTED, whose recorded figure is a LOWER BOUND on what
// completing would have cost -- unbounded above, and approximately equal to whatever ceiling
// stopped it. Every derivation below then consumed those bounds as costs: the band histogram
// counted them, `run_worst_cpu` folded them as maxima, and `identity_inflation_permille` divided
// one by another to produce a contention ratio.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATOR WAS PRESENT AND UNREAD, WHICH IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THE CLASS. The artifact
// has always carried a `verdict_reached` column; `claim_cost_columns` below resolved `identity`,
// `module`, `cpu_ms` and `eval_steps` by name, refused correctly on a MISSING column, and never
// asked for it. A discriminator a consumer must remember to join on is a warning, not a wall.
//
// SO THE ARMS ARE THE SCHEMA NOW. The producing artifact emits disjoint columns per reading kind
// (`observed_cpu_ms` against `cpu_at_least_ms`), so an old-vintage artifact whose header carries
// the shared `cpu_ms` column REFUSES with a positively recognised cause -- the correct fail-closed
// answer, because reading those rows as costs is the defect. The vintage is UNSUPPORTED rather than
// unreadable: it is identified exactly, and this reader declines to reconstruct the split.
//
// THE ARM NAMES CARRY `CpuReading` BECAUSE THIS IS A DIFFERENT CONTRACT FROM THE MODELLED FACT,
// AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS FILE PROVED WHY THAT MATTERS. The authority for the modelled
// observation is `v2.workflow.claim_cost_observation`, whose `RightCensoredCost` carries a witness
// identity, BOTH clock axes, and the ceiling. This type is the artifact-side reading of one TSV
// row restricted to the CPU axis: `ClaimCostColumns` indexes no wall column at all, so this reader
// structurally CANNOT fill `wall_lower_bound_ms` -- and a projection that cannot carry the
// authority's fields is not that authority's type.
//
// THE EARLIER DRAFT SPELLED THESE ARMS `ObservedCost` AND `RightCensoredCost`, which gave one
// spelling two materially different declarations in one change -- DESIGN section 3's meaning fork,
// and section 2's failed decomposition, since net concepts had grown by re-invention. The comment
// naming the single authority sat two lines above the re-minting of that authority's concept.
// IT WAS ALSO THE CAUSE OF A DIAGNOSTIC THAT LOOKED LIKE A COMPILER BUG: this module does not
// import the authority, but with that module merely PRESENT IN THE RESOLVED POOL the bare name
// bound to the FOREIGN product ambiently and silently, so one arm widened to this coproduct and
// its sibling stayed the foreign bare product -- `Coproduct(ClaimCostReading) vs
// Product(RightCensoredCost)`. The asymmetry between the two arms was never arbitrary; it was the
// collision. `ObservedCost` had no pool competitor and so widened correctly BY LUCK, which is why
// BOTH arms are renamed here rather than only the one that broke.
//
// So the rule this type is obeying: a module that names another as the authority for a concept may
// not declare a type whose SPELLING already belongs to that authority. Either consume it, or carry
// a different name because it is a different contract. This is the second.
type ClaimCostReading
  = ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: Millisecond }
  | RightCensoredCpuReading { cpu_lower_bound_ms: Millisecond, censoring_ceiling_ms: Millisecond }

// The cost figure a derivation is ENTITLED to treat as a POINT, and `Absent` for a censored row.
// Every consumer asking a POINT question goes through this, so a bound cannot reach a site that
// treats it as an exact cost without the caller writing an `Absent` arm -- the same discipline the
// seed's `exact_cost_ms` enforces on the producing side.
//
// THE QUANTIFIER IS OVER POINT QUESTIONS AND NOT OVER ARITHMETIC, which is narrower than it first
// reads and deliberately so. `row_crossing` matches `RightCensoredCpuReading` directly and does compare
// its bound, because a CROSSING is a lower-bound question that a bound can legitimately answer.
// That is not a bypass of this accessor; it is the case split this module exists to force, and it
// carries a third `RowCrossingUndecidable` arm for the bounds that cannot answer. Writing the
// quantifier over every arithmetic site would have been the wider claim that this file's own
// crossing guard falsifies.
fn observed_cpu_ms(reading: ClaimCostReading) -> Millisecond? {
  match reading {
    ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: c } => Present { value: c }
    RightCensoredCpuReading { cpu_lower_bound_ms: _, censoring_ceiling_ms: _ } => none
  }
}

// The LOWER BOUND a row is known to be at or above, on either arm. An observed row's bound is its
// own cost. This is the one question a censored row CAN answer, and it is the only direction that
// is sound: "this row cost at least N" is true on both arms, "this row cost N" is true on one.
fn cpu_at_least_ms(reading: ClaimCostReading) -> Millisecond {
  match reading {
    ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: c } => c
    RightCensoredCpuReading { cpu_lower_bound_ms: b, censoring_ceiling_ms: _ } => b
  }
}

fn reading_is_censored(reading: ClaimCostReading) -> Bool {
  match reading {
    ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: _ } => false
    RightCensoredCpuReading { cpu_lower_bound_ms: _, censoring_ceiling_ms: _ } => true
  }
}

// BOTH AXES ARE CARRIED ON THIS ROW, AND NEITHER DERIVES THE OTHER. `verdict_reached`
// (gunbc#10209) says whether the claim ANSWERED; `cost` says whether its clocks are a MEASUREMENT
// or a right-censored LOWER BOUND. They come apart in both directions: a host panic reaches no
// verdict while its clocks are exact, because the unwind ended the evaluation. Collapsing them
// would mint a bound for a row that has a measurement, or a verdict for one that never answered.
type FloorCostRow {
  identity: String
  module: String
  verdict_reached: Bool
  cost: ClaimCostReading
  eval_steps: Int
}

// One run's population, named by its run id so a finding can cite which runs it rests on.
type RunCost {
  run: NonEmptyStr
  rows: List<FloorCostRow>
}

// COLUMNS ARE LOCATED BY NAME, NOT BY POSITION, AND THIS IS NOT STYLE.
//
// DESIGN section 3's standing rule -- cite the symbol, never the position -- applies to a TSV
// exactly as it applies to a source file, and for the same reason: any insertion ABOVE the cited
// index silently invalidates it. That is not hypothetical here. The `eval_steps` column was
// INSERTED into this artifact between vintages: run 33661252708's header is
// `identity module outcome verdict_reached wall_ms cpu_ms cost_line_ms` and run 33699540412's is
// the same with `eval_steps` before `cost_line_ms`. A parser keyed on index 6 reads the older
// artifact's `cost_line_ms` -- the constant 100 -- as a step count, which is not an error, it is a
// PLAUSIBLE NUMBER: the resulting microseconds-per-step figures came out near 2500 instead of near
// 2, off by three orders of magnitude and still perfectly well-formed. Measured, not imagined:
// three of eleven sampled runs produced exactly that before the header was consulted.
//
// So the header IS the schema and a missing column REFUSES. `Absent` here means "this artifact
// does not carry the column this analysis needs", which is a fact about the artifact and must
// reach the caller as one, rather than being defaulted into a zero or a constant that flows into
// every downstream percentile.
//
// AND THE COLUMN SET IS NOW PER READING KIND. `cpu_ms` no longer exists in the artifact: a
// completed row fills `observed_cpu_ms` and a preempted one fills `cpu_at_least_ms` beside its
// `censoring_cpu_limit_ms`. Resolving all three by name means an OLD-VINTAGE artifact -- one whose
// header still carries the shared `cpu_ms` -- refuses wholesale rather than being read as if every
// row were a completion. That refusal is the point and not a regression, because the previous
// behaviour of reading them all as costs is the defect this module was the live specimen of -- and
// the honest reason is that this reader DECLINES the legacy encoding, not that the bytes are
// silent. They carry `outcome` and `verdict_reached`, so a separating signal exists; reconstructing
// it is simply not work this version does.
type ClaimCostColumns {
  identity: Int
  module: Int
  verdict_reached: Int
  cost_reading: Int
  observed_cpu_ms: Int
  cpu_at_least_ms: Int
  censoring_cpu_limit_ms: Int
  eval_steps: Int
}

fn claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: List<String>, name: String) -> Int? {
  header_fields
    |> enumerate
    |> filter(e => e.second == name)
    |> map(e => e.first)
    |> first
}

fn parse_verdict_reached(value: String) -> Bool? {
  if value == "true" { Present { value: true } }
  else if value == "false" { Present { value: false } }
  else { none }
}

// THE HEADER LINE IS THE ONE THAT NAMES `identity` FIRST. The artifact's `#`-prefixed summary
// precedes it, and a data row can never be mistaken for it because no claim identity is the
// literal string "identity".
fn claim_cost_columns(header_line: String) -> ClaimCostColumns? {
  let fields = split(s: header_line, delimiter: "\t")
  match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "identity") {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: id } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "module") {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: md } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "verdict_reached") {
        Absent => none
        Present { value: verdict } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "cost_reading") {
          Absent => none
          Present { value: kind } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "observed_cpu_ms") {
            Absent => none
            Present { value: obs } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "cpu_at_least_ms") {
              Absent => none
              Present { value: bound } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "censoring_cpu_limit_ms") {
                Absent => none
                Present { value: ceil } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "eval_steps") {
                  Absent => none
                  Present { value: steps } => Present {
                    value: ClaimCostColumns {
                      identity: id,
                      module: md,
                      verdict_reached: verdict,
                      cost_reading: kind,
                      observed_cpu_ms: obs,
                      cpu_at_least_ms: bound,
                      censoring_cpu_limit_ms: ceil,
                      eval_steps: steps
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// A TSV LINE IS EITHER A ROW OR IT IS NOT, AND "NOT" IS NEVER A FABRICATED ROW. The artifact
// carries a `#`-prefixed summary line and a header line before the data, and a short or
// unparseable line is refused rather than defaulted to zero -- a zero would sit silently at the
// bottom of every band and inflate the safe population.
// THE READING KIND SELECTS WHICH COLUMNS ARE READ, and a row whose named kind does not match the
// columns it actually filled is REFUSED rather than repaired. A `right_censored` row with an empty
// bound column, or an `observed` row with an empty cost column, is an artifact defect: recovering
// a figure from the other arm's column would be exactly the promotion this module exists to stop.
// ONE FUNCTION PER READING KIND, EACH DECLARING `ClaimCostReading?` AS ITS OWN CONTRACT. The kind
// named in the row selects which columns are read, so the reading of those columns is the thing
// that differs and it is what earns a name -- `parse_claim_cost_reading` below is then a pure
// selection over the kind, with no column knowledge of its own.
//
// THIS ALSO REMOVES A DEPENDENCE ON BRANCH-JOIN INFERENCE, and that is worth stating rather than
// leaving as a silent consequence. When both arms were spelled inline, the whole-corpus strict
// path refused the join -- `if branches resolve to incompatible types: Coproduct(ClaimCostReading)
// vs Product(RightCensoredCpuReading)` -- while the entry-scoped path accepted the same bytes and ran
// them green. Each arm now widens against a DECLARED return type at a function boundary instead of
// against its sibling, which is a contract the checker is given rather than one it must infer.
// The acceptance-path disagreement itself is NOT diagnosed by this edit and is reported separately;
// re-spelling until the checker stops complaining would have been the authoring-time workaround
// DESIGN section 5 names, so this is a decomposition that stands on its own terms.
fn observed_cost_reading(fields: List<String>, columns: ClaimCostColumns) -> ClaimCostReading? {
  match parse_int(s: fields[columns.observed_cpu_ms]) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: cpu } => Present { value: ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: millisecond(count: cpu) } }
  }
}

// BOTH COLUMNS OR NEITHER. A censored row without its ceiling cannot say what stopped it, and a
// bound whose ceiling is unknown is not a reading this module can carry -- so a missing ceiling
// refuses the row rather than yielding a bound with a fabricated limit.
fn right_censored_cost_reading(fields: List<String>, columns: ClaimCostColumns) -> ClaimCostReading? {
  match parse_int(s: fields[columns.cpu_at_least_ms]) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: bound } => match parse_int(s: fields[columns.censoring_cpu_limit_ms]) {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: ceiling } => Present {
        value: RightCensoredCpuReading {
          cpu_lower_bound_ms: millisecond(count: bound),
          censoring_ceiling_ms: millisecond(count: ceiling)
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn parse_claim_cost_reading(fields: List<String>, columns: ClaimCostColumns) -> ClaimCostReading? {
  if fields[columns.cost_reading] == "observed" {
    observed_cost_reading(fields: fields, columns: columns)
  } else if fields[columns.cost_reading] == "right_censored" {
    right_censored_cost_reading(fields: fields, columns: columns)
  } else {
    none
  }
}

fn parse_claim_cost_line(line: String, columns: ClaimCostColumns) -> FloorCostRow? {
  if starts_with(s: line, prefix: "#") {
    none
  } else {
    let fields = split(s: line, delimiter: "\t")
    let widest = max_int(
      a: max_int(
        a: max_int(a: max_int(a: columns.identity, b: columns.module), b: columns.verdict_reached),
        b: max_int(a: columns.cost_reading, b: columns.eval_steps)
      ),
      b: max_int(a: max_int(a: columns.observed_cpu_ms, b: columns.cpu_at_least_ms), b: columns.censoring_cpu_limit_ms)
    )
    if count(fields) <= widest {
      none
    } else if fields[columns.identity] == "identity" {
      none
    } else {
      match parse_claim_cost_reading(fields: fields, columns: columns) {
        Absent => none
        Present { value: reading } => match parse_int(s: fields[columns.eval_steps]) {
          Absent => none
          Present { value: steps } => match parse_verdict_reached(value: fields[columns.verdict_reached]) {
            Absent => none
            Present { value: reached } => Present {
              value: FloorCostRow {
                identity: fields[columns.identity],
                module: fields[columns.module],
                verdict_reached: reached,
                cost: reading,
                eval_steps: steps
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn max_int(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  if a > b { a } else { b }
}

// AN ARTIFACT WHOSE HEADER LACKS A NEEDED COLUMN REFUSES, NAMING THE COLUMNS IT LACKED. The
// sentence that stood here said it YIELDS NO ROWS and told the caller to re-read
// `claim_cost_columns` if they wanted to tell an old vintage from an empty run -- both true of the
// partial-population version and both false now. A stale comment on a repaired implementation
// tells the next reader the old behaviour was intended, which is this change's own finding applied
// one turn later.
// A DATA LINE IS DECIDED BEFORE IT IS PARSED, SO THAT FAILING TO PARSE ONE IS A REFUSAL RATHER
// THAN AN ABSENCE. The artifact carries a `#`-prefixed summary line, a header, and possibly blank
// lines; none of those is a row and none of them is malformed. Everything else IS a declared row,
// and a declared row that does not parse is an artifact defect.
fn is_claim_cost_data_line(line: String) -> Bool {
  line != ""
    && !starts_with(s: line, prefix: "#")
    && !starts_with(s: line, prefix: "identity\t")
}

fn claim_cost_line_refuses(line: String, columns: ClaimCostColumns) -> Bool {
  match parse_claim_cost_line(line: line, columns: columns) {
    Absent => true
    Present { value: _ } => false
  }
}

// WHY THE CAUSE IS CARRIED AND LOCATED RATHER THAN COUNTED. A reader who is told only that N rows
// were dropped cannot act; a reader handed the offending line can. Each arm names a DIFFERENT
// artifact defect, and none of them is "the artifact was empty".
// THE CAUSE MUST NAME THE STATE ACCURATELY, NOT MERELY BE TYPED. An earlier revision called this
// arm `HeaderNotReadable`, which is a false diagnosis: the historical header is perfectly READABLE
// and is a KNOWN schema this version declines to support. A cause that misnames the state is the
// same defect as a name that misnames a behaviour -- the thing this change found in its own
// witness names -- and it is worse in a diagnostic, because the reader acts on it.
//
// `UnsupportedSharedCpuMsVintage` is separated from the generic arm because that vintage is
// RECOGNISED rather than merely unparsed: a header carrying `cpu_ms` and no `cost_reading` is the
// pre-split artifact, whose defining property is that one column held both populations. Naming it
// tells the operator to re-point the sample at a newer run; the generic arm cannot say that.
type ArtifactRefusalCause
  = NoHeaderLine
  | UnsupportedSharedCpuMsVintage { header_line: String }
  | UnsupportedArtifactSchema { header_line: String, missing_columns: List<String> }
  | MalformedDataRow { line: String }

// THE COLUMNS THIS READER REQUIRES, DECLARED ONCE so the refusal can name what was absent rather
// than reporting that something was.
data claim_cost_required_columns: List<String> = [
  "identity", "module", "verdict_reached", "cost_reading",
  "observed_cpu_ms", "cpu_at_least_ms", "censoring_cpu_limit_ms", "eval_steps"
]

fn missing_claim_cost_columns(header_line: String) -> List<String> {
  let fields = split(s: header_line, delimiter: "\t")
  claim_cost_required_columns |> filter(n =>
    match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: n) {
      Absent => true
      Present { value: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE PRE-SPLIT VINTAGE IS IDENTIFIED POSITIVELY, not inferred from failure: it carries `cpu_ms`
// and no `cost_reading`, which is exactly the shape in which a bound and a completion shared one
// column. This is also why the claim that the old bytes carry NO identifying signal is wrong --
// they carry this one, and they carry `outcome` and `verdict_reached` besides.
fn is_shared_cpu_ms_vintage(header_line: String) -> Bool {
  let fields = split(s: header_line, delimiter: "\t")
  match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "cpu_ms") {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: _ } => match claim_cost_column_index(header_fields: fields, name: "cost_reading") {
      Absent => true
      Present { value: _ } => false
    }
  }
}

// THE PARSE IS TOTAL: ROWS, OR A LOCATED REFUSAL. NEVER A SHORTER LIST.
//
// THIS TYPE EXISTS BECAUSE THE EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FUNCTION COMMITTED THIS PR'S OWN CLASS IN
// THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION. It returned `List<FloorCostRow>` and answered `[]` for a missing header,
// `[]` for an unreadable header, and silently DROPPED any line that failed to parse. Every derived
// figure downstream then stayed internally consistent over the rows that happened to survive, and
// nothing anywhere was countable as having gone wrong.
//
// That is DESIGN section 5's absorbing fallback wearing the opposite costume. The named form
// WIDENS when it cannot compute precisely -- rerun everything, scan all keys. This one NARROWS:
// the population silently shrinks and the analysis proceeds confidently over the survivors. Both
// substitute an answer for an admission of ignorance, and this direction is the harder one to see
// because a shrunken population produces no error, no warning, and no anomalous number.
//
// IT IS ALSO THE EXACT DEFECT THIS MODULE EXISTS TO PREVENT, ONE STAGE EARLIER. The coproduct
// above makes it unwritable to read a right-censored bound as an exact cost -- but a row that
// vanishes before it is judged never reaches the coproduct at all. A guarantee about how rows are
// classified is worth nothing without a guarantee that every declared row WAS classified.
//
// THE OBLIGATION, STATED SO A LATER EDIT CANNOT QUIETLY DROP IT: every line the artifact declares
// as a row receives a disposition -- parsed, or refused with its cause. A figure whose denominator
// is "the rows that parsed" is the bug.
type ClaimCostArtifact
  = ArtifactRows { rows: List<FloorCostRow> }
  | ArtifactRefused { cause: ArtifactRefusalCause }

fn parse_claim_cost_tsv(text: String) -> ClaimCostArtifact {
  let lines = split(s: text, delimiter: "\n")
  match lines |> filter(l => starts_with(s: l, prefix: "identity\t")) |> first {
    Absent => ArtifactRefused { cause: NoHeaderLine }
    Present { value: header } => match claim_cost_columns(header_line: header) {
      Absent =>
        if is_shared_cpu_ms_vintage(header_line: header) {
          ArtifactRefused { cause: UnsupportedSharedCpuMsVintage { header_line: header } }
        } else {
          ArtifactRefused {
            cause: UnsupportedArtifactSchema {
              header_line: header,
              missing_columns: missing_claim_cost_columns(header_line: header)
            }
          }
        }
      Present { value: columns } => {
        let data_lines = lines |> filter(l => is_claim_cost_data_line(line: l))
        match data_lines |> filter(l => claim_cost_line_refuses(line: l, columns: columns)) |> first {
          Present { value: bad } => ArtifactRefused { cause: MalformedDataRow { line: bad } }
          Absent => ArtifactRows {
            rows: data_lines |> flat_map(line => match parse_claim_cost_line(line: line, columns: columns) {
              Absent => []
              Present { value: row } => [row]
            })
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}


// THE BAND HISTOGRAM (question 1: is the population near the ceiling dense).
// `censored_rows` IS NOT DECORATION AND IT IS NOT A BAND. A right-censored row has no cost to
// place, and placing it by its bound would put every one of them in whichever band contains the
// ceiling -- manufacturing exactly the "population dense near the ceiling" shape this histogram
// exists to test for. So they are excluded from every band and counted once beside the whole
// histogram, because a histogram that silently drops rows is a selection view read as a
// population.
type CostBand {
  lower_ms: Millisecond
  upper_ms: Millisecond
  rows: Int
}

// COMPARISON GOES THROUGH `millisecond_count` RATHER THAN OVER THE CARRIER, because the carrier is
// what stops a figure read from one clock being compared against a figure read from another; the
// count is the projection a comparison is entitled to.
fn rows_in_band(rows: List<FloorCostRow>, lower_ms: Millisecond, upper_ms: Millisecond) -> Int {
  count(rows |> flat_map(r => match observed_cpu_ms(reading: r.cost) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: c } =>
      if millisecond_count(m: c) >= millisecond_count(m: lower_ms)
        && millisecond_count(m: c) < millisecond_count(m: upper_ms) { [r] } else { [] }
  }))
}

// THE CENSORED POPULATION, so that a caller rendering the histogram states what it left out. A
// band histogram whose bands sum to less than the population is only honest if the difference is
// reported, which is what this is for.
fn censored_rows(rows: List<FloorCostRow>) -> Int {
  count(rows |> filter(r => reading_is_censored(reading: r.cost)))
}

// ROWS KNOWN TO BE AT OR ABOVE A LINE, AND THIS ONE ADMITS BOTH ARMS ON PURPOSE. It asks a
// LOWER-BOUND question -- "is this row known to have cost at least N" -- which is exactly the
// question a censored row can answer soundly: it was stopped at its ceiling, so it is known to be
// at or above that ceiling. Including them here is not the promotion this module forbids; it is
// the one direction the bound supports. Contrast `worst_observed_cpu` below, which asks how much a
// row cost and therefore cannot admit them.
fn rows_at_or_above(rows: List<FloorCostRow>, ms: Millisecond) -> Int {
  count(rows |> filter(r => millisecond_count(m: cpu_at_least_ms(reading: r.cost)) >= millisecond_count(m: ms)))
}

fn cost_bands(rows: List<FloorCostRow>, edges: List<Millisecond>) -> List<CostBand> {
  enumerate(edges)
    |> flat_map(e =>
         if e.first + 1 < count(edges) {
           [CostBand {
              lower_ms: e.second,
              upper_ms: edges[e.first + 1],
              rows: rows_in_band(rows: rows, lower_ms: e.second, upper_ms: edges[e.first + 1])
            }]
         } else { [] })
}

// CROSSINGS AND THE COUNTERFACTUAL REPLAY.
//
// `excluded_modules` is what makes the replay possible: it answers "what would this run have done
// if these modules had been repaired", which is the only way to separate a repair's effect from
// the ambient contention of the run it landed on. It is NOT a claim that excluded rows cost zero;
// it is a claim about which rows could have crossed.
fn row_is_excluded(row: FloorCostRow, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> Bool {
  any(excluded_modules, m => m == row.module)
}

// A CROSSING IS A LOWER-BOUND QUESTION, so a censored row CAN answer it -- but only when its own
// bound clears the line being asked about, and that is a per-row fact rather than a standing one.
//
// "Did this row reach the line" is the one inference a bound supports, and excluding censored rows
// outright would under-count crossings and make the floor read healthier than it is -- the single
// direction this figure must never err in, since `runs_refused` is built on it. So they are
// admitted. What is NOT admitted is the assumption that admitting them is always sound.
//
// THE SOUNDNESS RESTS ON AN ORDERING BETWEEN TWO INDEPENDENTLY OWNED CONSTANTS, AND NOTHING
// ASSERTS IT. A censored row is known only to have cost at least its own bound. Whether that
// settles the question depends on how the bound compares to the line, and today it always does --
// the CPU safety ceiling (`v2.workflow.required_floor`
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`) sits well above the cost line the artifact records,
// so every censored row genuinely did cross. That is a COINCIDENCE OF TWO CONSTANTS IN DIFFERENT
// MODULES, not a property of the question: raise the cost line above the safety ceiling, or lower
// the ceiling, and every censored row would be silently counted as a crossing it never made, with
// `runs_refused` moving and nothing saying why.
//
// SO THERE ARE THREE ARMS AND THE THIRD IS COUNTED, NOT DEFAULTED. A censored row whose bound does
// NOT clear the line is UNDECIDABLE for this question, and it is refused and reported rather than
// resolved either way. Defaulting it to "crossed" is the tempting arm because it errs
// pessimistically -- and it is still the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids: it conflates
// "the safe answer" with "no answer", destroys the signal that the ordering has broken, and
// fabricates a fact about a row. That is the same argument this module already makes for refusing
// an inflation pair rather than dropping its censored member, applied to its own admission.
//
// TODAY THE THIRD ARM IS PROVABLY EMPTY, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING: it is a
// discriminating red that cannot currently fire, costs nothing while the ordering holds, and fires
// the moment it stops.
type RowCrossing
  = RowCrossed
  | RowBelowLine
  | RowCrossingUndecidable

// THE TWO CENSORED ARMS, SO THE BRANCH BELOW CAN BE READ WITHOUT RE-DERIVING THEM. When the bound
// CLEARS the line the row is known to be above it whatever its true cost was, which is a sound
// crossing. When the bound sits BELOW the line, the row is known only to be above the bound, the
// true cost may be on either side, and this module cannot tell -- so it refuses rather than
// guessing in either direction.
fn row_crossing(row: FloorCostRow, ceiling_ms: Millisecond) -> RowCrossing {
  match row.cost {
    ObservedCpuReading { cpu_ms: c } =>
      if millisecond_count(m: c) >= millisecond_count(m: ceiling_ms) { RowCrossed } else { RowBelowLine }
    RightCensoredCpuReading { cpu_lower_bound_ms: b, censoring_ceiling_ms: _ } =>
      if millisecond_count(m: b) >= millisecond_count(m: ceiling_ms) {
        RowCrossed
      } else {
        RowCrossingUndecidable
      }
  }
}

// The crossing verdict over one run, with the undecidable population carried BESIDE the crossers
// rather than folded into or out of them. Both lists are identities, so a caller can name the rows
// it could not decide instead of reporting a count it cannot act on.
type RunCrossingCensus {
  crossed: List<String>
  undecidable: List<String>
}

fn run_crossing_census(run: RunCost, ceiling_ms: Millisecond, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> RunCrossingCensus {
  let kept = run.rows |> filter(r => !row_is_excluded(row: r, excluded_modules: excluded_modules))
  RunCrossingCensus {
    crossed: kept |> flat_map(r => match row_crossing(row: r, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms) {
      RowCrossed => [r.identity]
      RowBelowLine => []
      RowCrossingUndecidable => []
    }),
    undecidable: kept |> flat_map(r => match row_crossing(row: r, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms) {
      RowCrossed => []
      RowBelowLine => []
      RowCrossingUndecidable => [r.identity]
    })
  }
}

fn run_crossings(run: RunCost, ceiling_ms: Millisecond, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  run_crossing_census(run: run, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms, excluded_modules: excluded_modules).crossed
}

// THE UNDECIDABLE POPULATION ACROSS A SAMPLE, so a caller reporting `runs_refused` can state what
// that verdict could not see. A refusal that is counted but never surfaced is the same silence as
// no refusal at all.
fn runs_crossing_undecidable(runs: List<RunCost>, ceiling_ms: Millisecond, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  runs |> flat_map(run => run_crossing_census(run: run, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms, excluded_modules: excluded_modules).undecidable)
}

// THE WORST COST IS A POINT QUESTION AND CANNOT ADMIT A BOUND, which is the opposite ruling from
// `run_crossings` directly above, over the same rows, for a reason worth stating rather than
// leaving to be re-derived: "how much did the worst row cost" has an ANSWER that a censored row
// does not carry, while "did any row reach the line" has a THRESHOLD that it does.
//
// So the result is typed. Folding bounds in with costs would return a number that is a maximum of
// two different quantities and reads as a cost; folding them out silently would report a maximum
// over the rows that COMPLETED, which is `censored_estimator_drops_its_own_tail` exactly -- a
// statistic over the survivors of a threshold on the very variable being estimated, biased low,
// and biased low precisely where the analysis is looking. Neither is available, so the arm names
// which one it is returning and how many rows it could not see.
type WorstCost
  = WorstObserved { cpu_ms: Millisecond }
  | WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: Millisecond, censored_rows: Int }

fn worst_cost_at_least_ms(w: WorstCost) -> Millisecond {
  match w {
    WorstObserved { cpu_ms: c } => c
    WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: b, censored_rows: _ } => b
  }
}

fn run_worst_cpu(run: RunCost, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> WorstCost {
  let kept = run.rows |> filter(r => !row_is_excluded(row: r, excluded_modules: excluded_modules))
  let censored = censored_rows(rows: kept)
  let bound = fold(
    kept,
    init: millisecond(count: 0),
    f: (worst, r) =>
      if millisecond_count(m: cpu_at_least_ms(reading: r.cost)) > millisecond_count(m: worst) {
        cpu_at_least_ms(reading: r.cost)
      } else { worst }
  )
  if censored > 0 {
    WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: bound, censored_rows: censored }
  } else {
    WorstObserved { cpu_ms: bound }
  }
}

// A FLOOR RUN REFUSES IF ANY ONE ROW CROSSES, so the run-level verdict is existential over rows
// and the fleet-level figure is a COUNT OF RUNS, never a count of rows: fifteen crossings in one
// run and one crossing in another are the same number of refused merges.
fn runs_refused(runs: List<RunCost>, ceiling_ms: Millisecond, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> Int {
  count(runs |> filter(run => count(run_crossings(run: run, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms, excluded_modules: excluded_modules)) > 0))
}

// THE CENSORING IS UNION-PROPAGATED ACROSS RUNS, not decided by whichever run happened to hold the
// maximum: if ANY sampled run contains a censored row, the fleet-wide worst is a lower bound, even
// when the largest figure came from a run that had none. A maximum is only exact when every member
// it ranged over was.
fn worst_over_runs(runs: List<RunCost>, excluded_modules: List<String>) -> WorstCost {
  let per_run = runs |> map(run => run_worst_cpu(run: run, excluded_modules: excluded_modules))
  let censored = fold(per_run, init: 0, f: (n, w) => match w {
    WorstObserved { cpu_ms: _ } => n
    WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: _, censored_rows: c } => n + c
  })
  let bound = fold(per_run, init: millisecond(count: 0), f: (worst, w) => {
    let v = worst_cost_at_least_ms(w: w)
    if millisecond_count(m: v) > millisecond_count(m: worst) { v } else { worst }
  })
  if censored > 0 {
    WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: bound, censored_rows: censored }
  } else {
    WorstObserved { cpu_ms: bound }
  }
}

// MAX-OVER-RUNS, the contention-independent ranking. Membership of any single run's crossing set is
// a function of that run's load as much as of the row, so a candidate population must be ranked on
// the worst each row has been observed to cost, never on one run's sample.
// BOTH MAXIMA CARRY THEIR CENSORING FOR THE SAME REASON `worst_over_runs` does, and it bites
// HARDEST here: this is the contention-independent RANKING that decides which producers become
// repair candidates, and a censored row's bound is approximately the ceiling -- the largest figure
// in the artifact. Reading those as costs ranks the rows the deadline stopped above the rows that
// are genuinely expensive, which inverts the candidate order the ladder is built from.
fn rows_max_cost(rows: List<FloorCostRow>) -> WorstCost {
  let censored = censored_rows(rows: rows)
  let bound = fold(rows, init: millisecond(count: 0), f: (w, r) =>
    if millisecond_count(m: cpu_at_least_ms(reading: r.cost)) > millisecond_count(m: w) {
      cpu_at_least_ms(reading: r.cost)
    } else { w })
  if censored > 0 {
    WorstIsLowerBound { at_least_ms: bound, censored_rows: censored }
  } else {
    WorstObserved { cpu_ms: bound }
  }
}

fn identity_max_cpu(runs: List<RunCost>, identity: String) -> WorstCost {
  rows_max_cost(rows: runs |> flat_map(run => run.rows |> filter(r => r.identity == identity)))
}

fn module_max_cpu(runs: List<RunCost>, module: String) -> WorstCost {
  rows_max_cost(rows: runs |> flat_map(run => run.rows |> filter(r => r.module == module)))
}

// THE REPAIR LADDER: what removing one more module actually buys, which is the quantity that
// decides whether per-producer repair is the right unit of work.
type LadderStep {
  removed: String
  worst: WorstCost
  runs_refused: Int
}

fn repair_ladder(
  runs: List<RunCost>,
  ceiling_ms: Millisecond,
  already_excluded: List<String>,
  order: List<String>
) -> List<LadderStep> {
  enumerate(order)
    |> map(e => {
         let excluded = concat(already_excluded, order |> take(n: e.first + 1))
         LadderStep {
           removed: e.second,
           worst: worst_over_runs(runs: runs, excluded_modules: excluded),
           runs_refused: runs_refused(runs: runs, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms, excluded_modules: excluded)
         }
       })
}

// RUN-TO-RUN INFLATION, PAIRED ON IDENTITY.
//
// CARRIED IN PERMILLE AS AN Int RATHER THAN AS A RATIO, deliberately. The quantity is a ratio, but
// an exact integer permille is comparable and orderable without a float, and the percentile below
// is an ORDER STATISTIC -- it selects an observed value and never interpolates between two, so no
// figure it returns is one this repository did not measure.
//
// `min_base_ms` exists because the artifact records whole milliseconds: at a 2ms baseline a
// one-millisecond scheduling difference is a 50% "inflation" that says nothing about contention.
// Rows below the floor are dropped rather than smoothed.
// A RATIO NEEDS TWO COSTS AND A CENSORED ROW HAS NONE, SO THE PAIR REFUSES. This is the
// derivation the class was caught on: a bound divided by a cost, or a cost by a bound, produces a
// perfectly well-formed permille figure whose value is decided by WHERE THE CEILING SITS. It then
// flows into `permille_percentile` and `implied_clean_run_budget`, so one promoted bound moves a
// derived budget. Receipt: gunbc#9629 measured one rendered 502ms figure denoting a completed cost
// of 2,902ms and another of 355ms -- a ratio built on either is wrong by a factor no coefficient
// recovers, which is why this refuses the PAIR rather than correcting it.
//
// THE REFUSAL IS PER PAIR AND THE DROPPED COUNT IS RETURNED, not silently filtered. Dropping
// censored pairs and reporting the percentile over the rest is the neighbouring failure exactly --
// the excluded rows are the expensive ones, so the survivors' statistic is biased low with no
// bound. A caller MUST report `censored_pairs` beside any percentile it quotes; that is what makes
// this an honest exclusion rather than a censored estimator.
// The two rows one identity contributes to a pairing. A named carrier rather than the builtin
// tuple `enumerate` yields, because that tuple has no constructor available here.
type InflationPair {
  base_row: FloorCostRow
  other_row: FloorCostRow
}

type InflationObservations {
  permille: List<Int>
  paired: Int
  censored_pairs: Int
}

fn inflation_pair_permille(b: FloorCostRow, o: FloorCostRow) -> Int? {
  match observed_cpu_ms(reading: b.cost) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: base_ms } => match observed_cpu_ms(reading: o.cost) {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: other_ms } =>
        Present { value: (millisecond_count(m: other_ms) * 1000) / millisecond_count(m: base_ms) }
    }
  }
}

// THE FLOOR IS APPLIED TO THE OBSERVED BASE ONLY. A censored base row has no figure to compare
// against `min_base_ms` at all, so it is not "below the floor" -- it is unpairable, and it is
// counted as such rather than disappearing into the same bucket as a cheap row.
fn identity_inflation(base: RunCost, other: RunCost, min_base_ms: Millisecond) -> InflationObservations {
  let candidates = base.rows
    |> flat_map(b => match other.rows |> filter(o => o.identity == b.identity) |> first {
         Absent => []
         Present { value: o } => [InflationPair { base_row: b, other_row: o }]
       })
    |> filter(p => millisecond_count(m: cpu_at_least_ms(reading: p.base_row.cost)) >= millisecond_count(m: min_base_ms))
  let permille = candidates |> flat_map(p => match inflation_pair_permille(b: p.base_row, o: p.other_row) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: v } => [v]
  })
  InflationObservations {
    permille: permille,
    paired: count(candidates),
    censored_pairs: count(candidates) - count(permille)
  }
}

// The bare permille list, for a caller that has already read `identity_inflation`'s counts and is
// stating them. It exists so the percentile helpers keep a plain list to sort; it is deliberately
// NOT the entry point, because obtaining it without the counts is how the exclusion goes unstated.
fn identity_inflation_permille(base: RunCost, other: RunCost, min_base_ms: Millisecond) -> List<Int> {
  identity_inflation(base: base, other: other, min_base_ms: min_base_ms).permille
}

// An order statistic over the observed values: `percent` is 0-100 and the result is always a
// member of the input, so it is a measurement and not an interpolation.
fn permille_percentile(values: List<Int>, percent: Int) -> Int? {
  let sorted = values |> sort_by(v => v)
  let n = count(sorted)
  if n == 0 {
    none
  } else {
    let idx = (percent * n) / 100
    if idx >= n { Present { value: sorted[n - 1] } } else { Present { value: sorted[idx] } }
  }
}

// THE MARGIN THE MEASURED INFLATION IMPLIES: the largest clean-run cost that still lands under the
// ceiling after inflation. Integer division floors, which rounds the budget DOWN -- the safe
// direction for a limit.
fn implied_clean_run_budget(ceiling_ms: Millisecond, inflation_permille: Int) -> Millisecond {
  if inflation_permille <= 0 {
    ceiling_ms
  } else {
    millisecond(count: (millisecond_count(m: ceiling_ms) * 1000) / inflation_permille)
  }
}

// INFLATION WITH THE WORK CONFOUND REMOVED BY CONSTRUCTION, WHICH IS WHY IT NEEDS NO CONTROL.
//
// `identity_inflation_permille` above pairs a row against itself across two runs, which is already
// the right pairing -- but a row whose cost moved because the TREE changed is inflation and cost
// change mixed, and separating them normally means choosing a control population and defending
// the choice. Every such choice made in gunbc#10094 had to be defended and two were withdrawn.
//
// Restricting the pairing to rows whose `eval_steps` are EQUAL removes the confound instead of
// modelling it: the evaluator executed the same number of steps in both runs, so the only thing
// that could have changed did not, and any remaining cpu movement is inflation. Reach is not
// assumed -- `count` the result against `identity_inflation_permille`'s to see what fraction of
// the population the discriminator covers, because a clean instrument over three rows is not a
// measurement of a corpus.
//
// TWO ROWS CAN SHARE A STEP COUNT WITHOUT SHARING WORK, and the honest statement of what this
// establishes accounts for it: equal steps means equal EVALUATOR work, not equal host work. A row
// that spends its time inside one opaque host call reports few steps in both runs and is admitted
// here while its real variation lives outside the evaluator. `v2.workflow.required_floor` already
// carries a declared rung drop for exactly that region, so the population this instrument is
// cleanest over is the one that is CPU-bound in the evaluator.
fn identity_inflation_permille_at_equal_work(
  base: RunCost,
  other: RunCost,
  min_base_ms: Millisecond
) -> List<Int> {
  base.rows
    |> flat_map(b => match other.rows |> filter(o => o.identity == b.identity && o.eval_steps == b.eval_steps) |> first {
         Absent => []
         Present { value: o } => [InflationPair { base_row: b, other_row: o }]
       })
    |> filter(p => millisecond_count(m: cpu_at_least_ms(reading: p.base_row.cost)) >= millisecond_count(m: min_base_ms))
    |> flat_map(p => match inflation_pair_permille(b: p.base_row, o: p.other_row) {
         Absent => []
         Present { value: v } => [v]
       })
}

// AND THE EQUAL-WORK PAIRING HAS ITS OWN REASON TO REFUSE A CENSORED MEMBER, beyond the shared
// one: an interrupted row's `eval_steps` is itself quantised by the deadline poll rather than a
// true count, so equality of step counts across a censored and a completed row does not mean the
// evaluator did the same work. The discriminator this function rests on is not available on that
// arm, so admitting one would defeat the very confound removal it exists for.

// HOW MANY RUNS ONE IDENTITY CROSSED IN, which is the question that separates a property of the
// ROW from a property of the RUN.
type CrossingRecurrence {
  identity: String
  runs_crossed: Int
}

// THE RECURRENCE JOIN. A ceiling that refuses the whole run makes "which rows crossed" a per-run
// set, and reading one such set says nothing about cause: a row crosses because it is expensive,
// because the run was slow, or both. Joining the sets across runs decides it. An identity that
// crosses in many runs is expensive and tightening the line reaches it; identities that each cross
// once and never again are being selected by the run, and the line is adjudicating something the
// tree does not control.
//
// IT DOES NOT DECIDE THE QUESTION ON ITS OWN, and the confound is not hypothetical. Runs sampled
// across different tree vintages have had crossing families REPAIRED between them, so an identity
// that stops recurring may have been fixed rather than never re-selected. A recurrence census is
// evidence about cause only over runs whose relevant population did not change, and a caller
// spanning vintages owes that qualification with the result.
// SORTED-SCAN GROUPING, NOT A NESTED FILTER. The first version folded the crossing ids and
// re-filtered the whole list for every unseen one, which is quadratic in the number of crossings,
// and appended to an accumulator with `concat`, which copies. Both are named in DESIGN section 6's
// bare-minimum-cost rule, whose whole point is that "n is small here" is not a time-stable fact:
// the population is per-run crossers today because the floor is healthy, and a bad week is exactly
// when this function gets called on a large one.
//
// So the ids are sorted once and scanned once, runs of equal ids are counted in a single pass, and
// the accumulator is built by prepending and reversed at the end. The output order becomes
// identity order rather than first-appearance order, which is a stronger property than the one it
// replaces -- a census whose order depends on which run happened to be sampled first is not a
// census anyone can diff.
type RecurrenceScan {
  started: Bool
  current: String
  seen: Int
  done: List<CrossingRecurrence>
}

fn crossing_recurrence(
  runs: List<RunCost>,
  ceiling_ms: Millisecond,
  excluded_modules: List<String>
) -> List<CrossingRecurrence> {
  let crossers = runs
    |> flat_map(run => run_crossings(run: run, ceiling_ms: ceiling_ms, excluded_modules: excluded_modules))
    |> sort_by(id => id)
  let scanned = fold(
    crossers,
    init: RecurrenceScan { started: false, current: "", seen: 0, done: [] },
    f: (acc, id) =>
      if acc.started && acc.current == id {
        RecurrenceScan { started: true, current: acc.current, seen: acc.seen + 1, done: acc.done }
      } else if acc.started {
        RecurrenceScan {
          started: true,
          current: id,
          seen: 1,
          done: concat([CrossingRecurrence { identity: acc.current, runs_crossed: acc.seen }], acc.done)
        }
      } else {
        RecurrenceScan { started: true, current: id, seen: 1, done: [] }
      }
  )
  if scanned.started {
    reverse(concat([CrossingRecurrence { identity: scanned.current, runs_crossed: scanned.seen }], scanned.done))
  } else {
    []
  }
}

// THE POPULATION THAT DECIDES WHETHER A CEILING IS ADJUDICATING THE TREE OR THE RUN: identities
// observed crossing in more than one of the sampled runs. Empty means every crossing was
// single-run, which is the shape a run-level cause produces.
fn recurring_crossers(recurrence: List<CrossingRecurrence>) -> List<CrossingRecurrence> {
  recurrence |> filter(r => r.runs_crossed > 1)
}

// EVALUATOR-STEP DETERMINISM IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM COST DISTRIBUTION. CPU may move between
// attempts; completed work on one byte-identical head may not. Both observed heads are carried:
// artifact acquisition supplies them, and the comparison refuses before looking at rows when they
// differ. A caller therefore cannot turn two different trees into a plausible determinism result.
type SameHeadAttemptPair {
  left_head: NonEmptyStr
  left_attempt: NonEmptyStr
  left_rows: List<FloorCostRow>
  right_head: NonEmptyStr
  right_attempt: NonEmptyStr
  right_rows: List<FloorCostRow>
}

// CAUSE IS OPEN. The measured 15-row drift does not travel through map_keys or map_values in the
// affected v2 witness paths, and std.primitive_identity already classifies both as
// HostUnspecifiedOrder behind the determinism gate. That eliminates one tempting attribution; it
// does not establish another. The existing cache-purity oracle was also run on
// rust_crate_partition_witness.witness_partition_fold_unit_count_holds, one of the 15: cold and
// resolved-graph-cache-warm executions returned byte-identical 88-byte PASS output. That rules out
// semantic cache impurity for the specimen; it does NOT rule out a cache hit changing only the
// instrumentation count by itself. The two retained same-head artifacts disfavor that explanation
// independently: attempt 3 is LOWER for five drifting identities and HIGHER for ten, whereas a warm
// cache skipping work predicts one direction. The cross-attempt sum is nearly, but not exactly,
// conserved, so attribution movement remains live beside genuine work drift. CAUSE IS NOT
// ESTABLISHED. THE FIXED-COMPONENT OWNERSHIP FORK IS OPEN: it may be work each claim must genuinely
// perform, in which case a declared per-claim complexity envelope is the next question; or the same
// setup may be recomputed per claim, in which case the remedy is compute-once rather than a budget.
// `v2.workflow.floor_pure_producer_share` makes the second arm non-obvious rather than hypothetical:
// the Rust target-model producers were measured and WITHDRAWN because serving them made the
// produced-declaration consumers worse. That arithmetic predates this fixed-component observation
// and is a load-bearing prior to re-derive, not permission to re-enrol them by inference.
// This detector names the first divergent
// ROW available in the cost artifact (identity, module, attempts and both counts). The artifact carries no expression trace,
// so claiming a first divergent evaluator point here would fabricate precision the producer does
// not expose.
type EvalStepDeterminismViolation
  = AttemptHeadsDiffer {
      left_head: NonEmptyStr
      left_attempt: NonEmptyStr
      right_head: NonEmptyStr
      right_attempt: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | CompletedIdentityAbsent {
      identity: String
      module: String
      completed_attempt: NonEmptyStr
      absent_attempt: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | CompletedIdentityUnavailable {
      identity: String
      module: String
      completed_attempt: NonEmptyStr
      unavailable_attempt: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | CompletedIdentityStepsDiffer {
      identity: String
      module: String
      left_attempt: NonEmptyStr
      left_steps: Int
      right_attempt: NonEmptyStr
      right_steps: Int
    }

fn completed_left_violations(pair: SameHeadAttemptPair) -> List<EvalStepDeterminismViolation> {
  pair.left_rows
    |> filter(row => row.verdict_reached)
    |> flat_map(left => match pair.right_rows |> filter(right => right.identity == left.identity) |> first {
         Absent => [CompletedIdentityAbsent {
           identity: left.identity,
           module: left.module,
           completed_attempt: pair.left_attempt,
           absent_attempt: pair.right_attempt
         }]
         Present { value: right } =>
           if !right.verdict_reached {
             [CompletedIdentityUnavailable {
               identity: left.identity,
               module: left.module,
               completed_attempt: pair.left_attempt,
               unavailable_attempt: pair.right_attempt
             }]
           } else if left.eval_steps != right.eval_steps {
             [CompletedIdentityStepsDiffer {
               identity: left.identity,
               module: left.module,
               left_attempt: pair.left_attempt,
               left_steps: left.eval_steps,
               right_attempt: pair.right_attempt,
               right_steps: right.eval_steps
             }]
           } else { [] }
       })
}

// The reverse pass contributes only identities wholly absent on the left. A right-completed row
// paired with a left interruption was already emitted by the left-to-right pass only when the
// left completed, so handle that asymmetric case here without duplicating two completed rows.
fn completed_right_only_violations(pair: SameHeadAttemptPair) -> List<EvalStepDeterminismViolation> {
  pair.right_rows
    |> filter(row => row.verdict_reached)
    |> flat_map(right => match pair.left_rows |> filter(left => left.identity == right.identity) |> first {
         Absent => [CompletedIdentityAbsent {
           identity: right.identity,
           module: right.module,
           completed_attempt: pair.right_attempt,
           absent_attempt: pair.left_attempt
         }]
         Present { value: left } => if !left.verdict_reached {
           [CompletedIdentityUnavailable {
             identity: right.identity,
             module: right.module,
             completed_attempt: pair.right_attempt,
             unavailable_attempt: pair.left_attempt
           }]
         } else { [] }
       })
}

fn same_head_eval_step_violations(pair: SameHeadAttemptPair) -> List<EvalStepDeterminismViolation> {
  if pair.left_head != pair.right_head {
    [AttemptHeadsDiffer {
      left_head: pair.left_head,
      left_attempt: pair.left_attempt,
      right_head: pair.right_head,
      right_attempt: pair.right_attempt
    }]
  } else {
    concat(completed_left_violations(pair: pair), completed_right_only_violations(pair: pair))
  }
}

fn same_head_eval_steps_are_deterministic(pair: SameHeadAttemptPair) -> Bool {
  count(same_head_eval_step_violations(pair: pair)) == 0
}

// THE BETWEEN-RUN ENVELOPE: THE QUANTITY A BUDGET MAY BE SET FROM, AND THE ONE THE PAIRWISE
// INFLATION ABOVE IS NOT.
//
// `identity_inflation_permille` pairs ONE run against ONE other. Its percentiles are computed
// across ROWS inside a single pair, so they describe how unevenly one host-pair ratio lands over
// different rows -- they say nothing about how that ratio varies from run to run. Two runs are ONE
// SAMPLE of the between-run quantity and one sample has no spread at all, so a pairwise median is a
// POINT ESTIMATE dressed as a distribution. Reading it as fleet variance indexes the distribution
// over the wrong thing, and a budget set from it moves the cliff rather than closing it: if the
// admitted envelope set is wider than the two hosts sampled, the pairwise figure is a LOWER BOUND
// and the margin is WORSE than stated. The error direction is safe for a claim that the spread
// exists and UNSAFE for anyone using the number as a budget input.
//
// This family indexes over RUNS instead. One row's envelope is its max over N runs against its min
// over the same N, so N runs contribute N-1 degrees of freedom rather than one, and the percentile
// is taken over rows whose figure is already a between-run quantity.
//
// COMPLETENESS IS CHECKED AT IDENTITY GRAIN AND NEVER ASSUMED. `runs_observed` is carried on every
// envelope so a row missing from some run -- interrupted, or not in that vintage's plan -- is
// EXCLUDED by a predicate the caller can see rather than silently contributing a narrower envelope
// computed over however many runs happened to carry it. A row present in three of twelve runs would
// otherwise report a smaller spread than one present in all twelve and be indistinguishable from a
// genuinely stable row.
//
// A CENSORED ROW IS NEVER A COST. `verdict_reached == false` means the deadline fired and the figure
// is a lower bound describing when, not what the row costs; `v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt` measured
// that the bound and the true cost are essentially uncorrelated in that population. Such rows are
// dropped from the scan, which lowers `runs_observed` and therefore removes the identity through the
// completeness predicate rather than through a silent filter.
//
// `hottest_run` AND `coolest_run` NAME WHICH RUN ACHIEVED EACH EXTREME, and that pair is the whole
// reason this type is not just a min and a max. A spread is compatible with two different worlds:
// per-row jitter that lands on a different run every time, and a systematically hot or cold run
// that lands on the same one. A median run factor cannot separate them -- it is robust exactly
// where the envelope is driven -- so `run_extreme_census` below counts argmax and argmin instead.
// Under per-row jitter each run is hottest for about one Nth of the population; a run that is
// coolest for most of the corpus is a property of the machine and not of the tree.
//
// `work_invariant` IS TRUE when every observation of this identity reported the same `eval_steps`.
// Equal evaluator steps means the evaluator executed the same work in every run, so the remaining
// cpu movement is inflation rather than a tree change -- the same confound removal
// `identity_inflation_permille_at_equal_work` performs, carried per row so one scan serves both the
// raw and the controlled population instead of two near-identical folds. It means equal EVALUATOR
// work and not equal HOST work: a row that spends its time inside one opaque host call reports few
// steps in every run and is admitted here while its real variation lives outside the evaluator,
// which is the region `v2.workflow.required_floor` already carries a rung drop for.
type IdentityEnvelope {
  identity: String
  runs_observed: Int
  min_ms: Millisecond
  max_ms: Millisecond
  hottest_run: NonEmptyStr
  coolest_run: NonEmptyStr
  work_invariant: Bool
}

// THE TAGGED ROW CARRIES ITS OWN EXACT COST, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ENVELOPE UNABLE TO SEE A
// BOUND. This type was authored against the flat `FloorCostRow.cpu_ms` field that this change
// replaced with the `ClaimCostReading` coproduct, and the envelope is a POINT question -- a
// min/max over runs -- so a right-censored lower bound has no answer to give it. Projecting the
// cost at TAGGING time means the fold below receives only rows that have an exact cost, rather
// than receiving every row and being trusted to ask.
//
// THE `verdict_reached` FILTER IS NOT A SUBSTITUTE AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE. It very nearly
// excludes the censored population today, because a claim a safety deadline preempted also reaches
// no verdict -- but those are two independent axes by construction (a host panic reaches no verdict
// and its clocks are EXACT), and a guard that holds by an upstream coincidence is the incidental
// denominator this ledger already names. The exclusion is done on the axis it is about.
type RunTaggedRow {
  run: NonEmptyStr
  row: FloorCostRow
  cpu: Millisecond
}

// THE SCAN STATE IS A COPRODUCT AND NOT A `started: Bool`, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE THIS FAMILY
// DIFFERS FROM `crossing_recurrence`'S IDIOM ABOVE, DELIBERATELY. A flag-plus-fields accumulator
// needs a value for every field before the first row, and two of these fields are `NonEmptyStr`.
// The only sentinel available is `"" as NonEmptyStr`, which TYPECHECKS AND REFUSES AT RUNTIME --
// the cast is the sole constructor and carries no total unwrap -- so the unstarted state would be
// spelled with a value that cannot exist. Splitting the state removes the question: the empty scan
// has no run fields to invent. DESIGN section 4b(4): the invalid state has no constructor.
type EnvelopeScan
  = EnvelopeScanEmpty
  | EnvelopeScanOpen {
      current: String
      seen: Int
      lo: Millisecond
      hi: Millisecond
      lo_run: NonEmptyStr
      hi_run: NonEmptyStr
      steps: Int
      steps_equal: Bool
      done: List<IdentityEnvelope>
    }

// SORTED-SCAN GROUPING, for the reason `crossing_recurrence` above states: a fold that re-filters
// the whole population per identity is quadratic in the corpus, and the corpus here is every
// executed claim in every sampled run. The rows are sorted once and scanned once, and the
// accumulator is built by prepending and reversed at the end rather than by `concat`, which copies.
fn envelope_scan_close(current: String, seen: Int, lo: Millisecond, hi: Millisecond, lo_run: NonEmptyStr, hi_run: NonEmptyStr, steps_equal: Bool) -> IdentityEnvelope {
  IdentityEnvelope {
    identity: current,
    runs_observed: seen,
    min_ms: lo,
    max_ms: hi,
    hottest_run: hi_run,
    coolest_run: lo_run,
    work_invariant: steps_equal
  }
}

fn envelope_scan_open(t: RunTaggedRow, carry: List<IdentityEnvelope>) -> EnvelopeScan {
  EnvelopeScanOpen {
    current: t.row.identity,
    seen: 1,
    lo: t.cpu,
    hi: t.cpu,
    lo_run: t.run,
    hi_run: t.run,
    steps: t.row.eval_steps,
    steps_equal: true,
    done: carry
  }
}

fn identity_envelopes(runs: List<RunCost>) -> List<IdentityEnvelope> {
  let tagged = runs
    |> flat_map(run => run.rows
         |> filter(r => r.verdict_reached)
         |> flat_map(r => match observed_cpu_ms(reading: r.cost) {
              Absent => []
              Present { value: c } => [RunTaggedRow { run: run.run, row: r, cpu: c }]
            }))
    |> sort_by(t => t.row.identity)
  let scanned = fold(
    tagged,
    init: EnvelopeScanEmpty {},
    f: (acc, t) => match acc {
      EnvelopeScanEmpty => envelope_scan_open(t: t, carry: [])
      EnvelopeScanOpen { current: cur, seen: n, lo: lo, hi: hi, lo_run: cool, hi_run: hot, steps: st, steps_equal: same_work, done: prior } =>
        if cur == t.row.identity {
          let cpu = millisecond_count(m: t.cpu)
          let colder = cpu < millisecond_count(m: lo)
          let hotter = cpu > millisecond_count(m: hi)
          EnvelopeScanOpen {
            current: cur,
            seen: n + 1,
            lo: if colder { t.cpu } else { lo },
            hi: if hotter { t.cpu } else { hi },
            lo_run: if colder { t.run } else { cool },
            hi_run: if hotter { t.run } else { hot },
            steps: st,
            steps_equal: same_work && st == t.row.eval_steps,
            done: prior
          }
        } else {
          envelope_scan_open(
            t: t,
            carry: concat([envelope_scan_close(current: cur, seen: n, lo: lo, hi: hi, lo_run: cool, hi_run: hot, steps_equal: same_work)], prior)
          )
        }
    }
  )
  match scanned {
    EnvelopeScanEmpty => []
    EnvelopeScanOpen { current: cur, seen: n, lo: lo, hi: hi, lo_run: cool, hi_run: hot, steps: _, steps_equal: same_work, done: prior } =>
      reverse(concat([envelope_scan_close(current: cur, seen: n, lo: lo, hi: hi, lo_run: cool, hi_run: hot, steps_equal: same_work)], prior))
  }
}

// THE ADMISSIBLE POPULATION, STATED AS A PREDICATE RATHER THAN APPLIED INSIDE THE SCAN.
//
// `runs_expected` is the caller's own count of sampled runs, so an identity absent from any one of
// them is excluded by an identity-grain join and not by a count that could be satisfied by the
// wrong twelve observations. `min_ms` exists because the artifact records whole milliseconds: at a
// 2ms baseline a one-millisecond scheduling difference is a 50% "inflation" that says nothing about
// contention, so rows below the floor are dropped rather than smoothed.
fn complete_envelopes(
  envelopes: List<IdentityEnvelope>,
  runs_expected: Int,
  min_ms: Millisecond
) -> List<IdentityEnvelope> {
  envelopes |> filter(e =>
    e.runs_observed == runs_expected
      && millisecond_count(m: e.min_ms) >= millisecond_count(m: min_ms))
}

fn work_invariant_envelopes(envelopes: List<IdentityEnvelope>) -> List<IdentityEnvelope> {
  envelopes |> filter(e => e.work_invariant)
}

// Carried in permille as an Int for the reason `identity_inflation_permille` states: an exact
// integer permille is comparable and orderable without a float, and `permille_percentile` selects
// an observed member rather than interpolating, so no figure it returns is one this repository did
// not measure.
//
// A ZERO BASELINE HAS NO RATIO AND IS REFUSED RATHER THAN DEFAULTED. The artifact records whole
// milliseconds and a cheap row genuinely reports 0, for which max-over-min is undefined; returning
// 0 or 1000 there would place an unmeasurable row at the bottom of every percentile and silently
// enlarge the safe population, which is the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids. `Absent`
// reaches the caller as the fact it is. `complete_envelopes`' `min_ms` floor already excludes these
// from any admitted population, so on the analysis path this arm is unreachable and the refusal is
// the guarantee that it stays so.
fn envelope_permille(e: IdentityEnvelope) -> Int? {
  if millisecond_count(m: e.min_ms) <= 0 {
    none
  } else {
    Present { value: (millisecond_count(m: e.max_ms) * 1000) / millisecond_count(m: e.min_ms) }
  }
}

fn envelope_permilles(envelopes: List<IdentityEnvelope>) -> List<Int> {
  envelopes |> flat_map(e => match envelope_permille(e: e) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: v } => [v]
  })
}

// THE DISCRIMINATOR BETWEEN A PROPERTY OF THE ROW AND A PROPERTY OF THE RUN.
//
// Under per-row jitter every run is hottest for roughly one Nth of the population and coolest for
// roughly one Nth; a run that carries the extreme for most of the corpus is systematically hot or
// cold, and the verdict a ceiling returns is then a property of WHICH RUNNER PICKED UP THE JOB
// rather than of the tree. This is a CENSUS and not a test: it reports the concentration and names
// no threshold, because how much concentration disqualifies a cost verdict is an operator question
// and `v2.workflow.required_floor` is where that ruling lives.
type RunExtremeCount {
  run: NonEmptyStr
  hottest_for: Int
  coolest_for: Int
}

fn run_extreme_census(runs: List<RunCost>, envelopes: List<IdentityEnvelope>) -> List<RunExtremeCount> {
  runs |> map(run => RunExtremeCount {
    run: run.run,
    hottest_for: count(envelopes |> filter(e => e.hottest_run == run.run)),
    coolest_for: count(envelopes |> filter(e => e.coolest_run == run.run))
  })
}

// THE WORST BETWEEN-RUN INFLATION OBSERVED, which is the input the attention constant in
// `docs/design-rung-drops.md` is derived from. That row states its own revision condition -- the
// constant MUST be re-derived the moment a larger inflation floor is measured -- so this is the
// producer that decides whether it has been, and a caller re-runs it rather than trusting the
// digits in that prose.
//
// IT IS A FLOOR AND NOT THE INFLATION. The sampled runs are a subset of the admitted execution
// envelopes, so a wider sample can only find a larger value; the figure can never be read as a
// bound on what the fleet can do.
fn worst_envelope_permille(envelopes: List<IdentityEnvelope>) -> Int {
  fold(envelope_permilles(envelopes: envelopes), init: 0, f: (worst, v) => if v > worst { v } else { worst })
}
